# Decided
- Taken (trueqbit):
  - table_base.h: "hold the bases the kinds of table build on." ->
    "define what the different kinds of tables have in common." The
    reviewer's "define what the kinds of table share."; trueqbit asked for
    the plural and offered "types"/"have in common"; "kinds" kept, as
    "type" reads otherwise in C++ (ruling 15) - ordinary_table, query_view
    and virtual_table are C++ types; "have in common" over "share", which
    also reads as shared ownership. The file also holds
    table_primary_key_contains and is_single_table_primary_key, which are
    no bases.
  - triggers.h (1): "...only their bodies take." -> "...only triggers
    take." - lang_createtrigger.html: "Both the WHEN clause and the trigger
    actions may access elements of the row ... NEW.column-name and
    OLD.column-name"; WHEN is outside the body.
  - generated_always.h (6): "represent the constraint of a generated
    column." -> "represent the constraint that makes a column generated."
    - gencol.html: generated columns "may have NOT NULL, CHECK, and
    UNIQUE constraints, and foreign key constraints".
  - sync_order.h (14): "...after those it depends on." -> "...after those
    SQLite needs before it." - foreignkeys.html: "The parent key
    definitions of foreign key constraints are not checked when a table is
    created."; the code orders indexes and triggers after their table or
    view only.
- Kept (trueqbit):
  - dbo_name.h (2), against the reviewer's "name a database object after
    its mapped class." and Claude's lean for it: dbo_name represents the
    name the programmer supplies as a C++ reflection annotation on the
    struct; resolve_dbo_name's fallback to extract_type_identifier<T>() is
    how a missing annotation is read, not the file's reason. Claude took a
    fact about the code for a fact about the Purpose - a correction,
    deferred with the third one in handover-feedback-domain-sources.md.
  - db_objects.h (4), against "represent a schema by its database
    objects.": the tuple is the "how", sqlite_orm's reading of a schema
    (ruling 13's qualifier, as cte.h's "as a table"); the proposal says
    nothing a reader does not already know.
  - table_identifier.h (9), column_identifier.h (10): "by its name" is how
    SQL identifies a table or a column, the concept rather than the
    member (b).
  - algorithms/foreign_keys.h: "compute over the foreign keys of a
    schema." (b); the reviewer: the plural name opens it to a kind.
- Kept, as already ruled (not reopened): fts5_options.h (3, trueqbit's
  wording from PR A); default.h, collate.h (12, "constraint" against
  SQLite's "clause" - ruling 13); auxiliary.h (13, a "+" mark in R*Tree
